How Do skills work

I. The Skill Test
Instead of a pool of d6s, this system utilizes d20s scaled by training.
•	The Pool: Your Skill Level (1–5) equals the number of d20s you roll.
•	The Goal: Roll equal to or under your relevant Stat.
•	Successes (Actions): * Each die that is under your stat provides 1 Action.
o	Each die that matches your stat exactly provides 2 Actions.

________________________________________
II. Modified & Unskilled Tests
Environment, stress, or difficulty can shift the number of dice in your pool.
•	Positive Modifiers (+1 to +5): Add dice to your pool, increasing the potential number of actions.
•	Negative Modifiers (-1 to -5): Remove dice from your pool.
•	The Difficult Test: * If your dice pool is reduced to 0, -1, or -2, or if the Storyteller allows an Unskilled Roll (Level 0), you must perform a Difficult Test.
o	The Roll: Roll 2d20 and take the lower result of the two.
________________________________________
III. Item Durability and Decay
Items in New Avalon are often experimental, worn, or operating under extreme stress. They possess a Durability Factor representing their remaining "life."
User Failure (The 20)
If you are using an item and one of your dice (the primary die in a standard roll or the kept die in a Difficult Test) results in a 20, the item loses 1 Level of Durability.
•	Example: An experimental pistol with Durability 3 becomes a paperweight after the third time you roll a 20 while firing it.
Target Failure (The 1)
When attacking an opponent equipped with defensive gear (like experimental armor), you can "break" their equipment through precision or luck.
•	If your attacking die results in a 1, the defender's item loses 1 Level of Durability.
•	Example: If you roll a 1 twice against a target wearing Electrical Defense Armor (Durability 2), their shielding short-circuits and fails completely.
________________________________________
IV. Mechanical Impact of Actions
Because a single skill roll can generate multiple actions (especially with the "Exact Stat" bonus), combat and tasks become highly efficient.
•	1 Action: Standard movement, a single strike, or a simple interaction.
•	2+ Actions: Multiple attacks in a single turn, or performing a complex task (like repairing an engine) in a fraction of the time.
